# Not committed, deliberately. CI installs with `pip install -e ".[dev]"`,
# which ignores lockfiles entirely -- so a committed uv.lock cannot fail, and
# a thing that cannot fail cannot guard. Worse, it answers the wrong question
# confidently: it records a resolved SHA for every git dependency, including
# any that pyproject.toml leaves on a moving ref, so an auditor asking "are
# our git deps pinned?" reads the lockfile and concludes yes. pyproject.toml
# is the single source of truth for that, and is where the pins live.
# The deliberate change that would make a lockfile meaningful here is moving
# CI to `uv sync --locked` (or adding a `uv lock --check` step); do that
# first, then commit the lock.
uv.lock
